<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  launchd USER agent for the vault spooler — the macOS counterpart to
  vault-spooler.service. STAGED, NOT INSTALLED: copy it into place only when you
  want the Mac draining on its own.

    sed "s|__HOME__|$HOME|g" ~/.claude/bin/vault-spooler.plist.example \
      > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.vault-spooler.plist
    launchctl load -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.vault-spooler.plist

  launchd has no %h, unlike the systemd unit next door, so the paths below are
  absolute and __HOME__ is substituted at install time rather than shipped
  pointing at whoever built it.

  Rollback, complete and immediate:
    launchctl unload -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.vault-spooler.plist
    rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.vault-spooler.plist

  Health:  bash ~/.claude/bin/vault-spooler-run.sh --status

  READING ITS STATUS. `launchctl list` will show last-exit 1 whenever the Mac is
  away from home, and that is not breakage: --once returns `0 if healthy or
  delivered else 1`, so 1 means "upstream was unreachable, everything retained".
  Expect it off-LAN. The real health signal is the `dead:` count from --status --
  that one means a note was REJECTED and needs a human. A StartInterval job is
  not throttled or disabled by a nonzero exit, so it keeps retrying on schedule.

  It calls the wrapper, not the spooler, so credentials stay in the 0600 env
  file and never land in this plist — a plist is world-readable by default.

  KeepAlive is deliberately absent. The spooler exits 0 when it has nothing to
  forward, and KeepAlive would turn that clean exit into a restart loop, which
  is the same trap vault-spooler.service documents for Restart=always. This runs
  on an interval instead: every 10 minutes, and once at load.

  What it can do if forgotten: PUT already-queued notes into a staging inbox a
  human reviews. The queue is bounded and refuses at enqueue rather than
  evicting, so this cannot grow a disk or delete anything.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>              <string>local.vault-spooler</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>__HOME__/.claude/bin/vault-spooler-run.sh</string>
        <string>--once</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>          <true/>
    <key>StartInterval</key>      <integer>600</integer>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>    <string>__HOME__/.claude/vault-queue/spooler.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>  <string>__HOME__/.claude/vault-queue/spooler.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
